WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:15.280 --> 00:00:22.050
[Music]

00:00:22.070 --> 00:00:25.920
[Narrator:] Have you ever stopped to take a look at people's hands?

00:00:25.940 --> 00:00:31.470
They can do many things.

00:00:31.490 --> 00:00:44.390
Some knit.

00:00:44.410 --> 00:00:46.870
Some help.

00:00:46.890 --> 00:00:53.350
Others point, and there are those that pound.

00:00:53.370 --> 00:00:58.730
Or they may repair watches.

00:00:58.750 --> 00:01:05.210
But at this moment these are the hands of action I want you to meet.

00:01:05.210 --> 00:02:22.040
[Sirens and music]

00:02:22.040 --> 00:02:25.960
[Narrator:] Exciting? Maybe so, but serious is the better word

00:02:25.980 --> 00:02:31.910
to describe the action that these two men train their hands for.

00:02:31.930 --> 00:02:37.200
There are people living who would say that these are the most important hands in the world,

00:02:37.220 --> 00:02:39.610
and if you will permit me a little boasting,

00:02:39.630 --> 00:02:43.560
I help them every now and then to train to do their work.

00:02:43.580 --> 00:02:56.560
[Music]

00:02:56.580 --> 00:03:01.500
Now you can tell immediately when Ed and Clarence are in a first-class argument.

00:03:01.520 --> 00:03:06.510
Ed sits as far over as he can in the driver's seat and frowns.

00:03:06.530 --> 00:03:11.110
Clarence sits over next to the other door and frowns.

00:03:11.130 --> 00:03:14.620
Their argument this time: at the scene of an accident,

00:03:14.640 --> 00:03:18.300
what are the most serious kind of injuries and what can be done

00:03:18.320 --> 00:03:22.700
to keep people alive until they are in safe hands at the hospital?

00:03:22.720 --> 00:03:33.810
[Music]

00:03:33.830 --> 00:03:36.060
[Doctor:] Thank you, Suzanne.

00:03:36.080 --> 00:03:41.780
[Music]

00:03:41.800 --> 00:03:47.170
Okay, fellows, so you don't agree about the right treatment for seriously injured people.

00:03:47.190 --> 00:03:50.510
To tell you the truth, I'm glad you don't.

00:03:50.530 --> 00:03:53.310
Your argument gives me a chance to give you some information

00:03:53.330 --> 00:03:56.680
that should clear up a lot of misunderstandings.

00:03:56.700 --> 00:04:00.980
To begin with, there is nothing new about injury.

00:04:01.000 --> 00:04:06.550
Since the beginning of time, man has had to care for injuries to his body.

00:04:06.570 --> 00:04:11.510
The injury received by a caveman who loses a battle with a dinosaur

00:04:11.530 --> 00:04:14.420
and the injury received because of an accident,

00:04:14.440 --> 00:04:20.300
say with a forklift truck, are alike in all but one way.

00:04:20.320 --> 00:04:26.550
What is different is that today's people are able to get better treatment for their injuries.

00:04:26.570 --> 00:04:30.970
You are concerned with getting the injured person from the place he was injured

00:04:30.990 --> 00:04:36.380
to the place where he can get this improved treatment in the best possible condition.

00:04:36.400 --> 00:04:39.630
The care and transportation of injured people

00:04:39.650 --> 00:04:43.440
must be done by you without causing further injury.

00:04:43.460 --> 00:04:47.420
I want you to think about four critically important points.

00:04:47.440 --> 00:04:51.030
Four things that you could see on your next emergency run.

00:04:51.050 --> 00:04:54.980
They are, one, blocked airway.

00:04:57.310 --> 00:05:04.200
Breathing passages which become blocked and make breathing difficult or impossible.

00:05:05.640 --> 00:05:08.800
Two, bleeding.

00:05:09.960 --> 00:05:14.030
Three, open wounds.

00:05:17.210 --> 00:05:19.750
Four, last of all, broken bones.

00:05:19.800 --> 00:05:25.140
Now, laboratories have a way of giving us the whys and wherefores of almost any problem.

00:05:25.170 --> 00:05:28.020
So let's see what we can uncover about these four.

00:05:28.040 --> 00:05:30.460
Let's step right over into this next room.

00:05:30.480 --> 00:05:32.610
Right after you, gentlemen.

00:05:32.630 --> 00:05:40.910
Clarence, Ed, I think you'll be interested in this.

00:05:48.260 --> 00:05:54.070
Well fellas, an airway is the passage leading from the nose and mouth to the lungs.

00:05:54.090 --> 00:06:00.630
Now when it becomes blocked, the flow of air to the lungs is slowed down or stopped.

00:06:00.650 --> 00:06:04.450
If this occurs, the injured person may die in just a few minutes.

00:06:04.470 --> 00:06:06.560
There are many things that can block airways.

00:06:06.580 --> 00:06:10.940
Sometimes the items are swallowed, such as a button,

00:06:10.960 --> 00:06:13.290
a piece of steak...you name it,

00:06:13.310 --> 00:06:15.130
it's been found in someone's lungs.

00:06:15.150 --> 00:06:20.510
More often than not the obstruction is water, vomit, or blood.

00:06:20.530 --> 00:06:26.850
Any of these objects or materials can cause a man to die from lack of air

00:06:26.870 --> 00:06:31.440
just the same as if he had been smothered with a blanket.

00:06:31.460 --> 00:06:35.390
This is how a person breathes.

00:06:35.410 --> 00:06:43.130
See how the air flows freely to the lungs and returns.

00:06:43.150 --> 00:06:52.660
Now let's see, I'll use a golf tee, and believe me, golf tees have been found lodged in throats.

00:06:52.680 --> 00:06:59.040
This will be our object here, right in the throat.

00:06:59.040 --> 00:07:05.680
There now, close up, you see? The stage is set for suffocation.

00:07:05.700 --> 00:07:13.530
Now, when the object is removed, breathing can then become normal again.

00:07:13.550 --> 00:07:16.490
Now frequently, the obstruction is bleeding and is located

00:07:16.510 --> 00:07:19.700
so that the blood flows into the airway.

00:07:19.720 --> 00:07:23.920
This can occur when a fractured face breaks a blood vessel,

00:07:23.940 --> 00:07:26.480
or if the person vomits,

00:07:26.500 --> 00:07:30.910
or when a rib breaks a blood vessel, or in many other ways.

00:07:30.930 --> 00:07:34.940
It's worse if he's placed on his back.

00:07:34.960 --> 00:07:43.180
See how the blood could run from a broken nose right into the lungs?

00:07:43.200 --> 00:07:48.490
Muscles of the throat are arranged so that any object in the throat

00:07:48.510 --> 00:07:57.300
causes them to tighten up and prevents the air from being inhaled into the lungs.

00:07:57.320 --> 00:08:01.550
Well, the solution to the problem of a blocked airway is simple--

00:08:01.570 --> 00:08:04.930
remove the obstruction; don't let it happen again.

00:08:04.950 --> 00:08:08.920
Suzanne, would you bring in Blondie?

00:08:08.940 --> 00:08:13.450
All right now, to remove a small object the well-known methods

00:08:13.470 --> 00:08:21.750
of pounding on the back, and turning upside down. Sorry.

00:08:21.770 --> 00:08:28.630
These will usually work, but, well, it can be dangerous if there are other injuries,

00:08:28.650 --> 00:08:33.900
and may even cause more damage to the person by these methods.

00:08:33.920 --> 00:08:39.040
Clarence, could you help me out? Sit up here, please.

00:08:39.060 --> 00:08:44.380
All right come on over and bring this arm down and over on your side.

00:08:44.400 --> 00:08:51.560
Get your shoulder up here...

00:08:51.580 --> 00:08:56.620
Now, the best way to take the object out of the airway

00:08:56.640 --> 00:09:01.700
is to reach in with your fingers and pull it out.

00:09:01.720 --> 00:09:09.380
This is particularly true when the object is visible; not lodged deep in the throat.

00:09:09.400 --> 00:09:16.330
Have the injured man on his side and open his mouth like this.

00:09:16.350 --> 00:09:20.880
If you can see the obstruction, then pull it out.

00:09:20.900 --> 00:09:24.820
If the material is blood, vomit, or something like that,

00:09:24.840 --> 00:09:32.020
then turn his head to the side and clear his mouth in this way.

00:09:32.040 --> 00:09:36.140
One of the most valuable services you can perform for an injured person

00:09:36.160 --> 00:09:40.010
is to prevent his airway from becoming blocked.

00:09:40.030 --> 00:09:44.050
If you see bleeding from the mouth, nose, eyes, ears,

00:09:44.070 --> 00:09:48.150
or for some other reason you suspect that the injured person

00:09:48.170 --> 00:09:55.720
may have internal bleeding, then he should be transported like this on his side.

00:09:55.740 --> 00:10:01.700
Okay, Clarence, thank you. Now, fellows, let's think about bleeding.

00:10:01.720 --> 00:10:11.560
[Music]

00:10:11.580 --> 00:10:14.800
How much blood do you have in your body?

00:10:14.820 --> 00:10:18.710
Normally the body contains five quarts.

00:10:18.730 --> 00:10:23.480
If two to three quarts are lost, a person usually dies.

00:10:23.500 --> 00:10:26.580
To show you how much blood I'm talking about, watch this.

00:10:26.600 --> 00:10:54.840
[Music]

00:10:54.860 --> 00:10:58.580
A person can lose this much blood and live.

00:10:58.600 --> 00:11:01.090
If he loses more, he may die.

00:11:01.110 --> 00:11:03.140
By cutting a large artery,

00:11:03.160 --> 00:11:08.460
a person can lose that amount in just about ten minutes.

00:11:08.480 --> 00:11:11.200
Now, this is why it's so important for you,

00:11:11.220 --> 00:11:15.520
Clarence and Ed and all of our attendants, to treat bleeding quickly.

00:11:15.540 --> 00:11:19.670
Now how does this blood move about in the body, circulate?

00:11:19.690 --> 00:11:23.570
All right, let's use this model and we'll demonstrate.

00:11:23.590 --> 00:11:27.470
Now the heart is the pump of our circulatory system.

00:11:27.490 --> 00:11:32.240
When the heart beats, it tightens up and blood squeezes from it

00:11:32.260 --> 00:11:34.880
and through the blood vessels of the body.

00:11:34.900 --> 00:11:38.510
As blood leaves the heart, it pulses through the artery.

00:11:38.530 --> 00:11:41.460
Now they are the pipelines that carry the blood from the heart

00:11:41.480 --> 00:11:43.420
to the rest of the body.

00:11:43.440 --> 00:11:49.120
In the arteries, the blood is bright red and moves in spurts and jumps.

00:11:49.140 --> 00:11:51.280
It then travels through the capillaries,

00:11:51.300 --> 00:11:55.450
which are very, very small blood vessels.

00:11:55.470 --> 00:11:58.750
The last step in the circulation process is the return

00:11:58.770 --> 00:12:02.400
of the blood to the heart through the veins.

00:12:02.420 --> 00:12:04.400
When the blood travels through the veins,

00:12:04.420 --> 00:12:10.200
it moves in a smooth-flowing stream and it has a dark red color.

00:12:10.220 --> 00:12:13.340
If we cut an artery, blood comes out in spurts.

00:12:13.360 --> 00:12:17.190
If a vein is cut, it flows out smoothly.

00:12:17.210 --> 00:12:19.760
Now fellas, remember that bleeding can occur

00:12:19.780 --> 00:12:23.300
inside the body as well as on the surface.

00:12:23.320 --> 00:12:27.570
It's important that you recognize bleeding and know how to stop it.

00:12:27.590 --> 00:12:32.370
First, in medical schools and in hospitals, bleeding is a common event.

00:12:32.390 --> 00:12:35.940
Let's use a film from our medical school to look at bleeding.

00:12:35.960 --> 00:12:46.010
[Music]

00:12:46.030 --> 00:12:52.080
This is actual bleeding from an artery and a vein.

00:12:52.100 --> 00:12:56.560
Notice the hole in the artery made by the scissors.

00:12:56.580 --> 00:13:01.400
It bleeds bright red blood in spurts.

00:13:01.420 --> 00:13:09.930
The bleeding can be stopped by direct pressure with a finger over the hole.

00:13:09.950 --> 00:13:15.190
The vein bleeds dark red blood in a steady stream.

00:13:15.210 --> 00:13:19.150
It, too, can be stopped by direct pressure.

00:13:19.170 --> 00:13:26.090
Arteries spurt bright red; veins flow dark red.

00:13:26.110 --> 00:13:29.890
Both can be stopped by pressure on the bleeding area.

00:13:29.910 --> 00:13:36.430
[Music]

00:13:36.450 --> 00:13:39.830
Let me show you something else about bleeding and its control.

00:13:39.850 --> 00:13:41.210
Come on over here.

00:13:41.230 --> 00:13:48.350
[Music]

00:13:48.370 --> 00:13:54.990
All right, now this represents the wound, the puncture.

00:13:57.260 --> 00:14:01.340
The water flowing from this hose can be compared to bleeding.

00:14:01.360 --> 00:14:04.410
You can put your hand over it to stop it.

00:14:04.430 --> 00:14:08.520
You can pinch the hose above the hole.

00:14:08.540 --> 00:14:10.070
That'll stop it.

00:14:10.090 --> 00:14:16.580
Or you can take some adhesive tape and try to get control.

00:14:16.600 --> 00:14:18.770
Here there we are. See, that'll stop it.

00:14:18.790 --> 00:14:26.720
[Music]

00:14:26.740 --> 00:14:30.460
Now these are the same methods you can use to control bleeding.

00:14:30.480 --> 00:14:32.970
Let me show you how these methods work.

00:14:32.990 --> 00:14:39.190
Suzanne, may I have that railroad picture?

00:14:39.210 --> 00:14:43.180
Now this accident occurred when the trainman missed his footing,

00:14:43.200 --> 00:14:49.140
fell, and cut his arm on a broken jar.

00:14:49.160 --> 00:14:52.010
The ambulance was called by a radio telephone

00:14:52.030 --> 00:14:57.190
and we will see how the attendants controlled severe bleeding.

00:14:57.210 --> 00:15:02.970
They first find out what the injury is and the man's general condition.

00:15:02.990 --> 00:15:06.400
When they are satisfied that there is no worse injury than the bleeding,

00:15:06.420 --> 00:15:09.710
they will try to stop it.

00:15:09.730 --> 00:15:13.510
There is the jar that did it.

00:15:13.530 --> 00:15:15.710
That's an artery bleeding.

00:15:15.730 --> 00:15:19.090
See how the blood gushes out?

00:15:19.110 --> 00:15:27.910
To stop the bleeding, he will apply direct pressure on the cut using a gauze pad and his hand.

00:15:27.930 --> 00:15:31.320
Notice here that these pads are too small.

00:15:31.340 --> 00:15:35.360
He needs a much larger one.

00:15:35.380 --> 00:15:41.890
The pale face shows that he has lost a lot of blood and he may be in shock.

00:15:41.910 --> 00:15:46.430
They are now wrapping an elastic bandage tightly over the gauze pad.

00:15:46.450 --> 00:15:50.980
This keeps the bleeding under control.

00:15:51.000 --> 00:15:56.810
Watch him give the bandage a twist occasionally. See?

00:15:56.830 --> 00:16:03.560
He does this to keep the edges tight.

00:16:03.580 --> 00:16:08.360
He also pulls on the elastic bandage to put pressure on the wound.

00:16:08.380 --> 00:16:11.720
The bandage should be very tight.

00:16:11.740 --> 00:16:14.910
They keep wrapping until the bleeding stops.

00:16:14.930 --> 00:16:41.580
[Music]

00:16:41.600 --> 00:16:47.480
With the bleeding stopped, the attendants carry the injured man to the ambulance and then to a hospital.

00:16:47.500 --> 00:16:56.780
[Music]

00:16:56.800 --> 00:17:00.440
I'll talk about the trouble with tourniquets in a minute,

00:17:00.460 --> 00:17:03.710
but first let's review some points.

00:17:03.730 --> 00:17:07.150
How did the men know it was an artery that was cut?

00:17:07.170 --> 00:17:09.770
[Ed:] The blood was bright red and was coming out in spurts.

00:17:09.790 --> 00:17:15.360
[Doctor:] That's right. Good.

00:17:15.380 --> 00:17:17.870
He remembered that when the blood left the heart

00:17:17.890 --> 00:17:21.030
and traveled through the arteries it was under pressure

00:17:21.050 --> 00:17:24.050
and moved along in jumps or spurts.

00:17:24.070 --> 00:17:25.970
Had it been a vein that was cut,

00:17:25.990 --> 00:17:29.950
then the blood would have been darker and would have flown smoothly.

00:17:29.970 --> 00:17:33.200
Another fact you remember is that the pressure was applied

00:17:33.220 --> 00:17:35.400
above the wound in the case of an artery,

00:17:35.420 --> 00:17:39.030
since the blood was flowing from the heart to the wound,

00:17:39.050 --> 00:17:40.250
and if it's in a vein,

00:17:40.270 --> 00:17:41.580
then to stop the bleeding,

00:17:41.600 --> 00:17:46.070
he would apply the pressure below the wound.

00:17:46.090 --> 00:17:48.730
Now, about tourniquets.

00:17:48.750 --> 00:17:49.790
Suzanne, would you help me?

00:17:49.810 --> 00:17:56.440
I want to demonstrate this. Roll up your sleeve.

00:17:56.460 --> 00:18:01.450
A tourniquet should be applied only when other methods fail.

00:18:01.470 --> 00:18:06.790
Remember this, if nothing else works, then you apply the tourniquet

00:18:06.810 --> 00:18:10.370
to an arm or a leg.

00:18:13.400 --> 00:18:22.270
All right, that represents our tourniquet, and we have the wound here, you see...

00:18:22.290 --> 00:18:31.880
And then we twist it until the blood flow ceases.

00:18:31.900 --> 00:18:33.650
There's an important rule to remember here.

00:18:33.670 --> 00:18:35.030
Very important.

00:18:35.050 --> 00:18:38.670
Do not release the tourniquet once it is applied.

00:18:38.690 --> 00:18:42.900
This is a job to be done at the hospital.

00:18:42.920 --> 00:18:47.990
Thank you, Suzanne.

00:18:48.010 --> 00:18:51.740
Another possible injury is an internal hemorrhage;

00:18:51.760 --> 00:18:56.080
this is bleeding that is hidden or contained inside the body.

00:18:56.100 --> 00:18:59.360
With these injuries we may not see any blood,

00:18:59.380 --> 00:19:03.310
or even if we do, it may be only a small amount.

00:19:03.330 --> 00:19:05.560
A hemorrhage in the lungs often occurs

00:19:05.580 --> 00:19:11.740
when the body slams into something like a dashboard or a wall.

00:19:11.760 --> 00:19:16.500
May I have this picture?

00:19:16.520 --> 00:19:19.610
If the injured person is placed on his back,

00:19:19.630 --> 00:19:21.410
blood will collect in his lungs,

00:19:21.430 --> 00:19:29.650
and it is possible for him to drown in his own blood.

00:19:29.670 --> 00:19:33.280
The best position to place someone bleeding from the nose or mouth

00:19:33.300 --> 00:19:38.710
is lying on his side with his mouth lower than his lung.

00:19:38.730 --> 00:19:43.970
This may not appear to be a comfortable position but believe me it's the best one.

00:19:43.990 --> 00:19:48.320
Placing a person bleeding from the nose or mouth on his side

00:19:48.340 --> 00:19:56.210
may be the most important thing you will do to save his life.

00:19:56.230 --> 00:20:01.670
Now fellows, let's talk about a situation I'm sure you've faced more often than you like.

00:20:01.690 --> 00:20:05.310
It involves wounds that are severe and open.

00:20:05.330 --> 00:20:07.550
They require immediate attention.

00:20:07.570 --> 00:20:11.240
Ed, would you put up our next point, please, on the board?

00:20:11.260 --> 00:20:15.240
The danger with open wounds is that infection may follow.

00:20:15.260 --> 00:20:17.890
So clean dressings, clean hands,

00:20:17.910 --> 00:20:27.430
and avoiding touching open wounds are the things to remember.

00:20:27.450 --> 00:20:31.620
Does this look familiar? It ought to.

00:20:31.640 --> 00:20:35.180
Now, if you've ever put a patch on one of these like this,

00:20:35.200 --> 00:20:39.160
then of course you have some idea of how a bandage works.

00:20:39.180 --> 00:20:45.200
What we're doing when we use a bandage is applying a temporary patch on the body

00:20:45.220 --> 00:20:48.190
to prevent dirt from getting into the wound.

00:20:48.210 --> 00:20:54.630
Suzanne, would you give me that box?

00:20:54.650 --> 00:21:01.170
A sterile compress like this is the best thing to use for a bandage.

00:21:01.190 --> 00:21:11.050
There are other things you can use, such as adhesive tape or elastic bandages.

00:21:11.070 --> 00:21:15.230
The bandages used may vary from one type of wound to another,

00:21:15.250 --> 00:21:17.400
but the principle remains the same.

00:21:17.420 --> 00:21:22.860
Keep the wound clean, and to follow this rule, the things you need to do are,

00:21:22.880 --> 00:21:27.160
first of all, do not touch the wound with your hand.

00:21:27.180 --> 00:21:33.110
Secondly, cover the wound and the area around it with something that is clean.

00:21:33.130 --> 00:21:40.970
Third, keep the edges of the bandage tight against the skin so that dirt cannot get in.

00:21:40.990 --> 00:21:43.940
Now fellas, if you do not have a first-aid kit,

00:21:43.960 --> 00:21:48.750
a bandage can be anything that is clean that will do the job for you.

00:21:48.770 --> 00:21:54.360
For instance, well, a sheet, a cloth napkin,

00:21:54.380 --> 00:22:01.850
handkerchief, piece of clothing like this sweater.

00:22:01.870 --> 00:22:06.420
Of course, these things are not as ideal as the sterile compress.

00:22:06.440 --> 00:22:11.010
Now, let's examine situations in which applying bandages was necessary.

00:22:11.030 --> 00:22:13.210
Watch how the bandages are put on.

00:22:13.230 --> 00:22:15.990
Ed, would you put on the projector, please?

00:22:16.010 --> 00:22:29.220
[Music]

00:22:29.240 --> 00:22:33.780
The first thing the attendants do is examine all of the injured men

00:22:33.800 --> 00:22:41.410
and decide the order of treating them.

00:22:41.430 --> 00:22:43.540
This man has a sucking wound.

00:22:43.560 --> 00:22:49.150
It happened when he fell on a drill and punctured his chest.

00:22:49.170 --> 00:22:53.050
That torch on the scaffold caused this burn.

00:22:53.070 --> 00:22:55.320
Don't touch burns.

00:22:55.340 --> 00:23:00.980
The danger of infection is too great and you can't do anything to help.

00:23:01.000 --> 00:23:05.400
In spite of all the blood, this cut is not too serious,

00:23:05.420 --> 00:23:08.590
and can wait until the rest of the people have been examined

00:23:08.610 --> 00:23:12.400
and the worst of the injuries have been taken care of.

00:23:12.420 --> 00:23:16.510
A piece of sheet metal has cut through this man's belly.

00:23:16.530 --> 00:23:19.110
Those are his intestines sticking out.

00:23:19.130 --> 00:23:23.550
The attendant is raising his knees to relax the stomach muscle.

00:23:23.570 --> 00:23:27.700
This helps keep the pressure off the intestines and wound.

00:23:27.720 --> 00:23:30.170
Notice that someone remains with this man

00:23:30.190 --> 00:23:34.960
to help protect the wound until the attendants return.

00:23:34.980 --> 00:23:41.140
They must treat the sucking wound first.

00:23:41.160 --> 00:23:45.570
The air coming through the hole could cause two things.

00:23:45.590 --> 00:23:51.850
The lung will collapse, or dirt and trash could be sucked into the chest.

00:23:51.870 --> 00:23:53.810
This is why his injury is so urgent.

00:23:53.830 --> 00:23:58.940
[Music]

00:23:58.960 --> 00:24:07.390
First the hole is sealed with a piece of adhesive tape.

00:24:07.410 --> 00:24:17.650
Then a large sterile dressing is put over the patch and taped down tightly.

00:24:21.520 --> 00:24:25.500
The tape over the dressing should also help seal out air.

00:24:25.520 --> 00:24:31.960
The edges of the tape overlap each other and are pressed down tightly.

00:24:35.130 --> 00:24:40.770
The gasping for air is not unusual because only one lung is working.

00:24:42.580 --> 00:24:48.270
You can also expect bleeding from the nose or mouth like you have here.

00:24:48.290 --> 00:25:13.150
[Music]

00:25:13.170 --> 00:25:18.220
The next most serious injury is the intestine that is sticking out.

00:25:18.240 --> 00:25:23.730
Infection of this wound could kill this man so don't touch it.

00:25:23.750 --> 00:25:26.080
Leave the intestine out.

00:25:26.100 --> 00:25:32.320
Keep your hands off of it and apply a sterile dressing.

00:25:36.510 --> 00:25:41.120
Don't do anything that will cause more of the intestine to come out,

00:25:41.140 --> 00:25:45.350
like pushing on his chest or stomach.

00:25:45.370 --> 00:25:49.750
Paste the dressing tightly enough to hold the intestines still,

00:25:49.770 --> 00:25:54.390
but not tightly enough to push it back into the wound.

00:25:59.280 --> 00:26:05.510
Carry this man on his back; not on his side or face down.

00:26:05.530 --> 00:26:09.550
With burns like this there is nothing you can do.

00:26:09.570 --> 00:26:12.380
Infection is the greatest problem here,

00:26:12.400 --> 00:26:16.260
so keeping your hands off the burn is best.

00:26:16.280 --> 00:26:21.100
Don't put any dressing on. Take him to the hospital as he is.

00:26:22.450 --> 00:26:25.440
This cut only needs a sterile dressing.

00:26:25.460 --> 00:26:38.270
[Music]

00:26:38.290 --> 00:26:42.680
If possible, use a large dressing on these cuts.

00:26:42.700 --> 00:26:46.850
It's better to have one too large than one too small.

00:26:46.870 --> 00:26:50.490
This is the size which you should keep in your ambulance.

00:26:51.890 --> 00:26:56.510
Elastic bandage holds the dressing in place. Put it on tightly.

00:26:58.580 --> 00:27:04.020
Since there was no spurting blood, it's not likely that any major artery was cut.

00:27:05.830 --> 00:27:10.680
This type of injury often looks more serious than it really is.

00:27:10.700 --> 00:27:22.380
[Music]

00:27:22.400 --> 00:27:27.350
Now, let's review the steps these attendants followed in each case.

00:27:27.370 --> 00:27:31.130
They checked carefully to see what kind of wound they had to work with.

00:27:31.150 --> 00:27:33.670
Then they selected the correct bandage.

00:27:33.690 --> 00:27:38.010
They were careful not to touch the bandage where it touched the wound.

00:27:38.030 --> 00:27:43.070
Then the bandage was applied so that it overlapped the injured area.

00:27:43.090 --> 00:27:46.280
They did not put any ointment or medicine on the wound.

00:27:46.300 --> 00:27:48.520
They left that for the doctors to do.

00:27:48.620 --> 00:27:54.340
[Clarence:] Doc, what do we do when we find a person with something sticking in him?

00:27:54.370 --> 00:27:56.880
[Doctor:] Good question, Clarence.

00:27:56.900 --> 00:28:00.020
The answer is, leave it alone.

00:28:00.040 --> 00:28:06.750
If, as in the cowboy movies, you find an arrow or any other object sticking in a person,

00:28:06.770 --> 00:28:10.770
just take the person and the object to the doctor.

00:28:10.790 --> 00:28:15.080
Don't take it out or cram it in.

00:28:15.100 --> 00:28:17.070
A good rule to follow is this one:

00:28:17.090 --> 00:28:19.800
don't touch a wound if you don't have to.

00:28:19.820 --> 00:28:23.080
The bandages you put on will only last to the hospital.

00:28:23.100 --> 00:28:25.170
The doctors will strip them off.

00:28:25.190 --> 00:28:30.180
Your job is to see that the injured person arrives in as good a condition as possible,

00:28:30.200 --> 00:28:33.790
and not to tie up pretty bandages.

00:28:33.810 --> 00:28:39.620
Ed, would you put up our fourth and final point, broken bones?

00:28:39.640 --> 00:28:44.140
The broken bone or fracture is an injury that you will often see,

00:28:44.160 --> 00:28:47.890
but being common doesn't make it any less trouble to take care of.

00:28:47.910 --> 00:28:52.000
Broken bones can vary from an uncomfortable, inconvenient injury

00:28:52.020 --> 00:28:55.610
to a very serious injury requiring surgery.

00:28:55.630 --> 00:28:58.250
Bone injuries can and do cause death.

00:28:58.270 --> 00:29:03.920
You are sometimes in a position to save a life or a limb by doing the right thing.

00:29:03.940 --> 00:29:08.960
You can cause death if you're careless when handling broken bones.

00:29:08.980 --> 00:29:11.980
There are two main types of broken bones:

00:29:12.000 --> 00:29:16.640
closed fractures and open fractures.

00:29:20.740 --> 00:29:26.600
Now this represents the bone, and a closed fracture is one

00:29:26.600 --> 00:29:32.540
in which the sharp ends of the broken bone do not come through the skin.

00:29:32.570 --> 00:29:37.350
If the sharp ends of the broken bones do come through the skin,

00:29:37.370 --> 00:29:41.590
then it's an open fracture.

00:29:41.610 --> 00:29:47.370
Now, closed fractures can vary, quite a difference.

00:29:50.450 --> 00:29:58.220
Closed fractures can vary from a small crack in the bone, like this...

00:29:58.240 --> 00:30:05.620
all the way to a bone that is shattered.

00:30:05.640 --> 00:30:10.840
The usual symptoms of a broken bone are swelling,

00:30:10.860 --> 00:30:12.760
an unusual twist of the limb,

00:30:12.780 --> 00:30:14.380
tenderness, and pain,

00:30:14.400 --> 00:30:20.190
and a good way to find out if the person has a fracture is to ask him where it hurts.

00:30:20.210 --> 00:30:22.400
Now, an open fracture is more obvious,

00:30:22.420 --> 00:30:25.720
but it poses a different problem for you.

00:30:25.740 --> 00:30:30.780
The danger of infection is great because of the exposed sharp bone end.

00:30:30.800 --> 00:30:34.280
Even gangrene and loss of the limb could occur.

00:30:34.300 --> 00:30:37.350
There are two rules for the care of all fractures,

00:30:37.370 --> 00:30:39.810
and some special rules for open fractures.

00:30:39.830 --> 00:30:44.030
Now rule number one, splint all suspected fractures,

00:30:44.050 --> 00:30:47.200
and rule two, splint them where they lie;

00:30:47.220 --> 00:30:49.770
that is, where you find them.

00:30:49.790 --> 00:30:52.420
A splint can be any material that you want to use

00:30:52.440 --> 00:30:54.710
that will hold a broken bone in place,

00:30:54.730 --> 00:30:59.160
and there are reasons you immediately splint suspected fractures.

00:30:59.180 --> 00:31:04.930
First of all, a small amount of body movement can cause the sharp ends

00:31:04.950 --> 00:31:06.440
of the broken bone

00:31:06.460 --> 00:31:07.280
to punch through the skin,

00:31:07.300 --> 00:31:11.070
thus changing a closed fracture to an open fracture.

00:31:11.090 --> 00:31:14.300
Secondly, splinted fractures are less painful,

00:31:14.320 --> 00:31:18.840
and third, there is less damage to surrounding muscles and other tissues,

00:31:18.860 --> 00:31:23.560
so therefore, less shock to the patient when the fracture is splinted.

00:31:23.580 --> 00:31:27.530
A splinting is easy if you understand the idea.

00:31:27.550 --> 00:31:32.820
You put something rigid around the break to keep the bones from moving.

00:31:32.840 --> 00:31:35.240
Splinting materials are no trouble to find.

00:31:35.260 --> 00:31:38.400
For instance, well, we have packaged splints,

00:31:38.420 --> 00:31:43.770
limbs from trees, magazines, blankets, pillows

00:31:43.790 --> 00:31:47.400
are just some I can think of offhand.

00:31:47.420 --> 00:31:48.480
Let me show you this.

00:31:48.500 --> 00:31:53.050
Ed, bring over the blanket and the magazine.

00:31:53.070 --> 00:31:59.030
This'll give you a good idea here.

00:31:59.050 --> 00:32:04.120
Now, a blanket rolled from each end toward the center

00:32:04.140 --> 00:32:07.320
can be made into a splint like this.

00:32:07.340 --> 00:32:09.930
Of course it's wrapped.

00:32:09.960 --> 00:32:21.280
Magazines can be interfolded like this and then wrapped.

00:32:23.330 --> 00:32:28.010
Ed, would you get me that item leaning against the counter there?

00:32:28.030 --> 00:32:31.810
Over behind the skeleton there. That's a Thomas splint.

00:32:32.870 --> 00:32:35.430
This is a traction splint, fellows.

00:32:35.450 --> 00:32:37.780
Ed, I'm going to ask you to help me with the demonstration.

00:32:37.800 --> 00:32:40.520
Clarence, would you come around here?

00:32:40.540 --> 00:32:43.620
You should have two men for applying this splint.

00:32:43.640 --> 00:32:46.240
And it works by the pull that it puts on the limb.

00:32:46.260 --> 00:32:48.740
Ed, get up here on the table.

00:32:48.760 --> 00:32:58.100
Notice that it has this reversible metal ring.

00:32:58.120 --> 00:33:01.680
That means that you can use it on either leg.

00:33:01.700 --> 00:33:06.450
Also, this buckle should be on the outside.

00:33:06.470 --> 00:33:08.480
Good.

00:33:08.500 --> 00:33:12.870
Now the metal ring is placed underneath, snug against the pelvis,

00:33:12.890 --> 00:33:16.930
and of course, then this is buckled down very tightly and securely.

00:33:16.950 --> 00:33:19.770
Clarence, pull, pull very hard on that.

00:33:19.790 --> 00:33:22.980
That keeps the leg in traction.

00:33:23.000 --> 00:33:29.010
Now what we're going to do is, we're going to tie the leg

00:33:29.030 --> 00:33:31.770
to the bottom of the splint by using an ankle hitch.

00:33:33.900 --> 00:33:42.550
Regular knot, put this around under and loop it, and then this is tied,

00:33:44.470 --> 00:33:48.060
that's it, keep pulling on that now Clarence, that's tied to the end of the splint

00:33:48.080 --> 00:33:50.840
and that keeps the leg in traction.

00:33:50.860 --> 00:33:52.290
That's good. Very fine, thank you.

00:33:52.310 --> 00:33:54.340
Remember to leave the shoe on.

00:33:54.360 --> 00:33:55.750
Thank you, fellas.

00:33:55.770 --> 00:34:00.430
Applying the splint to the injury is not difficult at all.

00:34:00.450 --> 00:34:06.080
What you're doing is you're tying it down tightly enough so the bone will not move.

00:34:06.100 --> 00:34:10.710
I want you to look at some pictures that show the splinting of a fracture.

00:34:10.730 --> 00:34:14.620
Suzanne, would you turn on our projector again, please?

00:34:26.190 --> 00:34:29.440
This girl was injured on water skis.

00:34:29.460 --> 00:34:33.310
Her leg was fractured at the shin bone.

00:34:33.330 --> 00:34:37.390
The two most important rules about open fractures are,

00:34:37.410 --> 00:34:40.920
one, don't touch the wound or bone

00:34:40.940 --> 00:34:45.750
and, two, don't pull or push the bone back in.

00:34:45.770 --> 00:34:49.280
Leave it out.

00:34:49.300 --> 00:34:55.850
You should apply a large sterile compress to the wound to keep out dirt.

00:34:55.870 --> 00:35:01.530
Notice how he raises the leg to allow the other person to put the bandage on.

00:35:01.550 --> 00:35:07.460
Be gentle. If you pull very hard, the ends of the bone will go back in.

00:35:07.480 --> 00:35:12.410
This will happen to you a lot of times.

00:35:12.430 --> 00:35:18.230
The wrapping around the compress should be tight enough to hold it in place and stop bleeding,

00:35:18.250 --> 00:35:24.620
but not tight enough to push the bone back in.

00:35:24.640 --> 00:35:29.470
You should not use a Thomas splint on an open fracture below the knee;

00:35:29.490 --> 00:35:33.300
however, it should be used on any fracture of the thigh,

00:35:33.320 --> 00:35:39.330
but don't use too much traction if the bones stick through the skin.

00:35:39.350 --> 00:35:42.830
Here they used an oar for a splint.

00:35:42.850 --> 00:35:48.020
A stick or a tree limb would do as well.

00:35:48.040 --> 00:35:52.080
It's better to have a splint too long than one too short.

00:35:52.100 --> 00:35:58.310
You must keep the broken bone from moving.

00:35:58.330 --> 00:36:02.360
The splint is held firmly to the leg by elastic bandages,

00:36:02.380 --> 00:36:07.250
or any other wrapping material that will do the job.

00:36:07.270 --> 00:36:10.340
The pain with broken bones can be very severe,

00:36:10.360 --> 00:36:16.120
but good splinting can help and may prevent shock.

00:36:18.610 --> 00:36:25.750
You should remember this. The bandage is to keep out infection and stop bleeding.

00:36:25.770 --> 00:36:29.980
The splint is to keep the bone from moving.

00:36:30.000 --> 00:36:33.800
If you even think the bone is broken, splint it.

00:36:33.820 --> 00:36:43.260
[Music]

00:36:43.280 --> 00:36:51.160
This girl is ready to be moved.

00:36:51.180 --> 00:36:55.230
Well, fellows. You've seen the way to splint a fracture.

00:36:55.250 --> 00:37:00.090
I don't want to leave the subject of fractures without emphasizing that your most important act

00:37:00.110 --> 00:37:04.750
is to keep the broken bone from sticking through the skin.

00:37:04.770 --> 00:37:09.350
If you let this happen, you can't do enough things right to make up for the damage you've caused.

00:37:09.370 --> 00:37:12.330
Suzanne, let me have this, please.

00:37:15.150 --> 00:37:18.750
The open fracture is splinted identically to the closed fracture,

00:37:18.770 --> 00:37:23.600
but the special rule is to cover the exposed bone end with a clean dressing

00:37:23.620 --> 00:37:27.240
to prevent the further introduction of dirt and trash into the wound.

00:37:28.690 --> 00:37:31.330
Do not, and I repeat this,

00:37:31.350 --> 00:37:34.710
do not push bone ends back into the wound,

00:37:34.730 --> 00:37:39.620
as this carries dirt deep into the wound.

00:37:39.640 --> 00:37:46.250
Now, if the neck or the back is the suspected area of a fracture or broken bone,

00:37:46.270 --> 00:37:53.020
then you will have to use great care.

00:37:53.040 --> 00:38:01.570
This represents the backbone, called the spinal column, and it has the spinal cord running through it.

00:38:01.590 --> 00:38:07.810
Any damage to this cord can cause permanent paralysis or death.

00:38:07.830 --> 00:38:11.200
Now, if the bone or two is broken,

00:38:11.220 --> 00:38:18.360
and there's a possibility that any movement can cause the spinal cord to be damaged or injured,

00:38:18.380 --> 00:38:22.090
you must not let this happen.

00:38:22.110 --> 00:38:27.680
Clarence, would you get up here, please, and lie back straight?

00:38:27.700 --> 00:38:35.180
I want to show you the manner in which to handle a suspected fracture of the neck.

00:38:35.200 --> 00:38:37.050
Keep the head straight.

00:38:37.070 --> 00:38:38.870
Do not allow it to twist.

00:38:38.890 --> 00:38:44.760
The way to do this is to always have one person pull on the head and the chin

00:38:44.780 --> 00:38:47.020
while the others move the body.

00:38:47.040 --> 00:38:52.290
Place the fingers behind the head with the thumbs on the chin.

00:38:52.310 --> 00:38:54.790
Pull hard and keep the chin up.

00:38:54.810 --> 00:39:01.140
Do not move the head to the side or tilt it forward.

00:39:01.160 --> 00:39:02.900
Thank you, Clarence.

00:39:02.920 --> 00:39:06.150
Well, fellows, we talked about four very important points.

00:39:06.170 --> 00:39:14.550
Right now I want to talk about something else that's just as important.

00:39:14.570 --> 00:39:19.300
Oh, uh, I goofed again. Fellows, I have to go.

00:39:19.320 --> 00:39:22.340
Gee honey, I'm sorry, believe me. One second more.

00:39:22.360 --> 00:39:25.550
Fellows, I helped you. Would you mind helping me?

00:39:25.570 --> 00:39:28.110
With all these demonstrations we've had here in the lab,

00:39:28.130 --> 00:39:30.270
it's not in very good condition for tomorrow.

00:39:30.290 --> 00:39:32.200
So, would you mind giving Suzanne a hand?

00:39:32.220 --> 00:39:36.180
I'll see you tomorrow. Thank you.

00:39:36.380 --> 00:40:36.000
[Music]

